Interrupting Maxima must not corrupt its state (SF bug #5614)
=============================================================

A user interrupt -- Ctrl+C, or a frontend's interrupt -- can arrive between
any two instructions.  Maxima answers it by throwing back to its top level,
which runs the UNWIND-PROTECT cleanups on the way out.  Several pieces of
Maxima's own bookkeeping take more than one step, and an interrupt between two
of those steps used to leave a half that no cleanup undid.  The bug report
measured this on SBCL with real SIGINTs: 12% of interrupts of a loop of
function calls, 3% of an assume/forget loop.  A harness interrupting at random
moments found, before the fix, per 1000 interrupted runs:

  a loop of function calls with block variables
  (fpprec, simp, a parameter)                  350 corrupted (35%)
  the same with local()                        170 (17%)
  assume() and forget()                         50 (5%)
  fpprec:30, fpprec:16                         155 (15%)
  f(z):=z, kill(f)                              46 (5%)

and none of 1000 per case after it.  What went wrong, and what does it now:

- Block variables and function parameters (MBIND, MLAMBDA, MBINDING).  MBIND
  assigned a variable and then recorded the value to restore; MLAMBDA set a
  "setup finished" flag only after MBIND had returned and its cleanup undid
  nothing without it.  An interrupt in either gap left the variable at its
  local value for good -- simp:false or fpprec included -- and the binding
  stacks out of step with each other.  MBIND now records first, and drops the
  record again if the assignment never happened; the cleanups unwind the
  stacks to a mark taken on entry (MUNBIND-TO) instead of a count; and each
  entry is restored before it is popped, so an interrupted cleanup leaves it
  for the next one.

- local() (MLOCAL, MUNLOCAL).  A frame was pushed outside the UNWIND-PROTECT
  meant to pop it, and MUNLOCAL popped saved properties per variable but the
  variables only at the end.  The frame is now pushed inside, and taken down
  one variable at a time together with its saved properties.

- The top level.  An interrupt that lands in a cleanup abandons the rest of
  it; an outer cleanup finishes the job, but at the top there is none.
  CONTINUE and MACSYMA-TOP-LEVEL now unwind the stacks to where they stood
  when they started (UNWIND-MAXIMA-FRAMES) before reading each input.

- The fact database.  A fact is put on its context and on the nodes it
  mentions in one WITH-DATABASE-TRANSACTION; cut in half, it is in effect
  without facts() listing it, or the reverse.  A transaction now holds
  interrupts back.  LEARN-ABS and LEARN-NUMER switch to $LEARNDATA and back
  inside an UNWIND-PROTECT, so neither an interrupt nor an error leaves the
  session in the wrong context.

- fpprec.  FPPREC1 set the working precision and its constants, and MSET then
  stored $FPPREC; in between, bfloat() used a precision fpprec did not show.
  FPPREC1 now stores all of them, $FPPREC included, at once.

- Function definitions.  MDEFINE listed a function in functions before
  storing its definition, and kill() removed the definition well before the
  listing: functions could list a function that has none.  Both now change
  the two together.

The tool for "together" is WITH-INTERRUPTS-DEFERRED (src/clmacs.lisp), which
holds interrupts back until its body is done: SB-SYS:WITHOUT-INTERRUPTS on
SBCL, CCL:WITHOUT-INTERRUPTS, MP:WITHOUT-INTERRUPTS on ECL with threads, and
SYS:WITHOUT-INTERRUPTS on CMUCL.  Elsewhere it is a PROGN, and only the
ordering above helps.  It is for bookkeeping only: nothing in its body can be
interrupted, so it must never wrap user code, a question, or mathematics.
That is also why MSET is not wrapped as a whole: an ASSIGN property may run
user code (value_check), and $SETCHECKBREAK enters a break loop.

Not covered: kill() and remove() take many steps, and an interrupted kill()
can leave a symbol partly killed (each listing still agrees with its
property).  Parallel workers are a separate matter (README.parallel-*).

Tests, appended to tests/rtest_mset.mac and loading interrupt-safety-check.lisp:

- interrupt_deferral_check() sends the running thread an interrupt from inside
  WITH-INTERRUPTS-DEFERRED and checks it runs only after the body.
- interrupted_cleanup_check(function_call|block_variables|block_local) makes
  an ASSIGN property throw, as an interrupt would, while the cleanup restores
  the variable; it checks that the state was left inconsistent and that the
  top level's repair makes it whole again.  These are deterministic.
- interrupt_stress_check(expr, rounds) interrupts EXPR at random moments
  through Maxima's own debugger hook, the way Ctrl+C does, and checks the
  binding stacks, the restored values, fpprec, the context, facts() against
  is(), and functions.  It reports too_few_interrupts if under a quarter of
  the rounds were actually interrupted, rather than passing vacuously.

The threaded checks return skipped on a lisp without threads.  Against the
code before the fix (with WITH-INTERRUPTS-DEFERRED and UNWIND-MAXIMA-FRAMES
supplied as shims) the deterministic checks and all four stress checks fail.
Measured on SBCL 2.2.9 only.
